Feature Extraction Using TF-IDF
TF-IDF is a numerical statistic that reflects how important a word is to a document in a collection (corpus).
It is widely used in text mining, information retrieval, and NLP to convert text data into numeric feature vectors that machine learning algorithms can understand.
________________________________________
🧠 TF-IDF = TF × IDF
Let’s break it down:
1️⃣ Term Frequency (TF)
How frequently a term appears in a document.

2️⃣ Inverse Document Frequency (IDF)
Measures how unique or rare a word is across all documents.
 
➕ Combining both:
TF-IDF(t,d)=TF(t,d)×IDF(t)TF\text{-}IDF(t, d) = TF(t, d) \times IDF(t)TF-IDF(t,d)=TF(t,d)×IDF(t) 
________________________________________
⚙️ Why Use TF-IDF?
•	Highlights important terms in a review (e.g., "excellent", "boring") while ignoring common ones like "the", "and", etc.
•	Unlike Bag-of-Words (BoW), it reduces the weight of frequent but uninformative terms.
•	Produces sparse, high-dimensional vectors suitable for ML.
________________________________________
🛠️ TF-IDF in my Project:
Applied TF-IDF to transform the reviewText column into numerical features for training classifiers.
✅ Code Used:
python
from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer

tfidf = TfidfVectorizer(max_features=5000, stop_words='english')
X = tfidf.fit_transform(df['reviewText_clean'])
Explanation of Parameters:
•	max_features=5000: Keeps top 5,000 most important words.
•	stop_words='english': Removes common English stop words like “is”, “and”, “the”.
•	fit_transform: Fits the model to the text and transforms it into a matrix of TF-IDF features.
________________________________________
🔢 Output of TF-IDF
This results in a sparse matrix of shape (n_reviews, 5000).
Each row → one review
Each column → one of the 5,000 selected words
Each cell → the TF-IDF score of the word in that review


