GloVe (Global Vectors for Word Representation) is a popular unsupervised machine learning algorithm used for obtaining word embeddings. It creates dense vector representations of words based on their global context in a corpus of text. Here's a detailed breakdown of GloVe:
1. What is GloVe?
GloVe is a vector representation of words that captures their meaning by looking at the global co-occurrence statistics of a corpus. In simple terms, GloVe creates word vectors by analyzing how often words appear near each other in large text corpora.
Unlike Word2Vec, which only focuses on local context (i.e., words within a small window), GloVe takes into account global word-word co-occurrence across the entire corpus, making it more robust.
2. Why Use GloVe?
•	Capturing Global Context: GloVe captures both local and global word co-occurrences, making it better at capturing semantic relationships.
•	Pre-trained Models: GloVe provides pre-trained embeddings on large corpora like Wikipedia, Common Crawl, and others. These embeddings can be used for various NLP tasks without the need to train them from scratch.
•	Efficiency: GloVe is computationally efficient because it avoids scanning the entire context of words, unlike Word2Vec which focuses on nearby context.
3. Word Vectors & Semantic Relationships:
•	One of the key strengths of GloVe is that word vectors that are close in the vector space are semantically similar. For example, the word vectors for "king" and "queen" will be close, and the relationship between "man" and "woman" can be captured through vector arithmetic:
king−man+woman=queen\text{king} - \text{man} + \text{woman} = \text{queen} 
4. Applications of GloVe:
•	Text Classification: GloVe embeddings can be used as input features in classification models, such as sentiment analysis.
•	Similarity and Analogy Tasks: GloVe embeddings allow for measuring the similarity between words or finding word analogies.
•	Clustering: GloVe vectors can be clustered to group semantically similar words together.
•	Information Retrieval: GloVe can improve search engines by better understanding the semantic meaning of search queries and documents.
5. Advantages of GloVe:
•	Word Relationships: The word vectors obtained from GloVe reflect strong semantic and syntactic relationships.
•	Training Efficiency: It’s less computationally expensive compared to other models that require extensive training data, such as BERT.
•	Scalability: GloVe scales well to large datasets.
6. GloVe vs Word2Vec:
•	Word2Vec: Uses local context to predict words in a given window (skip-gram or CBOW models). It doesn’t explicitly use co-occurrence counts, making it more suited for capturing semantic relationships from local word contexts.
•	GloVe: Uses global co-occurrence statistics and factorizes the matrix of word co-occurrences, which helps it capture global word relationships and similarities better than Word2Vec.
7. Limitations of GloVe:
•	Fixed Embeddings: GloVe creates static embeddings, meaning they don't adapt to the context of the word in a sentence (unlike BERT).
•	Vocabulary Size: If a word is out-of-vocabulary, it cannot be represented, unlike contextual embeddings from models like BERT.
•	Computationally Intensive: The co-occurrence matrix generation can be computationally expensive, especially with large corpora.
8. GloVe Embeddings in Action:
•	You can use GloVe embeddings in NLP tasks by leveraging pre-trained word vectors or by training your own on a specific corpus.
•	For instance, in sentiment analysis, you can use GloVe word vectors as input features into machine learning models to classify sentiments in text.












Final Report: Sentiment Analysis using TF-IDF, GloVe Embeddings, and Logistic Regression
1. Project Overview
In this project, the goal was to perform sentiment analysis on a large dataset of product reviews, categorizing them into two classes: positive and negative. The analysis aimed to explore the effectiveness of different text representation techniques (TF-IDF and GloVe embeddings) and evaluate the performance of a Logistic Regression model for sentiment classification.
2. Dataset
•	The dataset used consists of customer reviews and ratings. The reviews are textual data, while the ratings are numerical.
•	Preprocessing Steps:
o	Removed HTML tags and irrelevant characters from the text using BeautifulSoup.
o	Lowercased the text and eliminated URLs and special characters using regular expressions.
3. Text Representation Techniques
The dataset was represented using two different text representation techniques:
•	TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency):
o	TF-IDF measures the importance of a word within a document relative to the frequency of the word across all documents.
o	It is effective at capturing important words and their context within the document. The top 5000 features were selected for vectorization.
•	GloVe (Global Vectors for Word Representation):
o	GloVe embeddings capture the semantic meaning of words by representing them as vectors in a high-dimensional space.
o	Pre-trained GloVe embeddings (available in 50, 100, 200, and 300-dimensional vectors) were used to represent the words in the reviews.
o	The average word embedding of each review was computed to create a vector for input to the machine learning model.
4. Model: Logistic Regression
•	Logistic Regression was chosen as the classification algorithm because it is simple, effective, and interpretable.
•	The model was trained on both the TF-IDF and GloVe embeddings representations of the review data.
•	SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) was applied to balance the dataset, addressing any class imbalance in the positive and negative classes.
5. Evaluation Metrics
The model was evaluated using the following metrics:
•	Precision: Measures the accuracy of positive predictions.
•	Recall: Measures the ability of the model to correctly identify positive instances.
•	F1-Score: The harmonic mean of precision and recall, giving a balanced measure of performance.
•	Accuracy: The overall correctness of the model in predicting the sentiment.
